<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Southern Liang</h1><p class="st-meta">397 CE – 414 CE · capital Ledu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the Tufa Xianbei in eastern Qinghai and the Hexi Corridor, which broke away from Later Liang in 397 and fell to Western Qin in 414.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Tufa were a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> clan of the same origin as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a>, settled around the Huangshui valley. In 397 their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tufa-wugu">Tufa Wugu</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang</a> and proclaimed himself king; the capital became Ledu in eastern Qinghai.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> was a frontier state of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> cavalry and Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people">Qiang</a> farmers. Under Tufa Rutan it acknowledged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a> and in 406 received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a>, the old Liang capital, but lost it after defeats by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a>. Rutan promoted Chinese officials and learning but waged costly wars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>While Rutan campaigned against the Yifu tribes in 414, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-qin">Western Qin</a> seized Ledu. He surrendered and was poisoned the next year. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tufa-poqiang">Tufa Poqiang</a> fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a>, where he became a noted general under the name Yuan He.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 397 CE – 414 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.60%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>397 CE – 414 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ledu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tufa Wugu, Tufa Lilugu, Tufa Rutan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liang (Tufa clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=406&amp;focus=southern-liang" aria-label="Southern Liang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7400 969 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Southern Liang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7616 1132h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7720 1225h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7720 1225h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=406&amp;focus=southern-liang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
